body:
Fixes #914 

Some recent changes introduced a bug when setting up the Transport config when running a server.

The code that changed and provoked this unintended behaviour is in `pkg/runner/config.go`: 
```
	// Check to see if the mcpTransport is defined in the metadata.
	// Use this value if it was not set by the user.
	// Else, default to stdio.
	if mcpTransport == "" {
		if imageMetadata != nil && imageMetadata.Transport != "" {
			logger.Debugf("Using registry mcpTransport: %s", imageMetadata.Transport)
			mcpTransport = imageMetadata.Transport
		} else {
			logger.Debugf("Defaulting mcpTransport to stdio")
			mcpTransport = types.TransportTypeStdio.String()
		}
	}
	// Set mcpTransport
	if _, err = c.WithTransport(mcpTransport); err != nil {
		return err
	}
```

This part of the code handles setting up the Transport to either:

- The given flag --transport, in which case the logic does not execute
- If registry imageMetadata is present, to whatever Transport is set there
- If not, defaults to stdio

The problem arises from the previous defaulting mechanism, in which the Transport was defaulted to stdio in the run command itself:
`runCmd.Flags().StringVar(&runTransport, "transport", "stdio", "Transport mode (sse, streamable-http or stdio)")
`

Because of this, the logic above never executed and acted as if the --transport flag was always given with "stdio" unless manually overridden.

This fixes the issue while keeping all the functionality intact.
